  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1048 AM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates a southwest flow aloft prevailing across the \nWestern High Plains ahead of an approaching upper level trough \nmoving into the Great Basin. Near the surface, a strengthening lee \nside trough of low pressure remains anchored in eastern Colorado.\n\nRelatively dry conditions are likely early in the period despite \nshort range ensembles indicating an upper level shortwave trough \nlifting northeast through the Central Rockies into the Northern High \nPlains. In response, lee side cyclogenesis is projected across the\nhigh plains of eastern Wyoming into eastern Montana tonight before\nlifting northeast into the western Dakotas Wednesday, allowing \nprevailing south-southwesterlies to persist across western Kansas,\ndoing little to increase moisture across the area and limiting \ninstability. A tightening pressure gradient ahead of the \naforementioned lee side trough in eastern Colorado will result in\ngusty winds persisting through early Wednesday before winds subside\ninto Wednesday afternoon as the approaching trough axis begins to\nwash out after moving into extreme southwest Kansas.\n\nWell above seasonal temperatures are forecast tonight as prevailing \nsoutherlies reinforce a milder air mass across the Western High Plains.\nWith the NBM showing a 70-90% probability of temperatures slipping\nbelow 50F by early Wednesday morning, look for lows generally down\ninto the 40s(F). A warming trend continues into Wednesday as prevailing\nsoutherlies continue to draw slightly warmer air northward into western\nKansas under clearing skies, pushing H85 temperatures up above 10C\nin central Kansas to near 15C in extreme southwest Kansas. Should\nsee widespread afternoon highs in the 60s(F) with the NBM pointing\nto a better than 90% probability of temperatures exceeding 60F.\nSimilar temperatures are likely Thursday.",
